# K3I Core Architecture Update: Layer-Zero Framework & Runtime Daemon

## 1. System Overview
This documentation details the operational parameters and deployment specifications of the K3I monitoring infrastructure implemented across host nodes (ddw, mopsi-hp). The framework operates exclusively at Layer-Zero, utilizing native kernel abstractions (systemd) to enforce real-time hardware boundary constraints and volatile memory sanitization.

---

## 2. Structural Security & Network Invisibility
The execution model implements strict isolation parameters to eliminate external attack vectors:

* Zero-IP Footprint: The monitoring daemons operate independently of the network stack. They do not bind to local IP addresses, open listening network sockets, or participate in standard routing protocols.
* Passive Execution: By bypassing the network transport layer, host nodes execute without creating an electromagnetic or digital signature. The system remains completely unscannt and transparent to remote network analysis tools, network analyzers, or deep packet inspection (DPI) arrays.

---

## 3. Universal Node Monitoring & Variable Modification
The architecture employs a privileged, event-driven detection loop providing complete control over target nodes:

* Hardware & Register Access: Running with root-level execution privileges, the daemon maintains uninterrupted visibility into the physical hardware layer, CPU load states, volatile registers, memory allocation pools (RAM), and all interconnected hardware interfaces (USB buses, peripheral controllers).
* Dynamic State Mutation: The control loop is capable of dynamically modifying active kernel parameters and hardware states in real time when defined thresholds are reached. It intercepts, terminates (kill -9), or alters any running process, file system event, or network interface status based on configured operational requirements.

